#### 1. **Filtering Accelerometer Data**

Let's start by smoothing the accelerometer data using a low-pass filter. The filter will help reduce noise and make the movement more stable.

```c
// Low-pass filter for accelerometer data smoothing
#define ALPHA 0.1f  // Smoothing factor, adjust as needed (lower = smoother)
int16_t smoothedX = 0, smoothedY = 0;

static Dir GetDirection(void) {
    int16_t buffer[3] = {0};
    int16_t xval, yval = 0x00;

    // Read the accelerometer data
    BSP_ACCELERO_GetXYZ(buffer);

    // Apply the low-pass filter to smooth out accelerometer values
    xval = (int16_t)(ALPHA * buffer[0] + (1.0f - ALPHA) * smoothedX);
    yval = (int16_t)(ALPHA * buffer[1] + (1.0f - ALPHA) * smoothedY);

    // Update smoothed values
    smoothedX = xval;
    smoothedY = yval;

    // Define thresholds for detecting significant movement
    if (xval > yval) {
        // Horizontal movement is more significant than vertical
        if (xval > ThresholdHigh) {
            return DIR_LEFT;   // Left direction
        } else if (xval < ThresholdLow) {
            return DIR_NONE;   // No movement
        } else {
            return DIR_UP;     // Vertical movement is stronger (Up direction)
        }
    } else {
        // Vertical movement is more significant than horizontal
        if (yval < ThresholdLow) {
            return DIR_NONE;   // No movement
        } else if (yval > ThresholdHigh) {
            return DIR_RIGHT;  // Right direction
        } else {
            return DIR_DOWN;   // Down direction
        }
    }
}
```

#### 2. **Integrating `GetDirection()` in Your Game Loop**

Once the accelerometer is smoothed and the direction is calculated, use this direction to control Pac-Man's movement. You'll modify your game loop to call `GetDirection()` instead of using joystick inputs.

